On Feb 9, 6:53 pm, derekt75 <derek.truesd...@anritsu.com> wrote:

> Starting with speed is quite nice.

Granted.  Poison resistance at level three is even better.

Actually, intrinsics are pretty nice for monks in general.
Telepathy is hard to get unless you break vegetarian
conduct, but you only have to take the alignment hit
*once* for that -- I don't believe I've ever eaten an eye
and failed to get the acuity.  I usually get fire and cold
resistances from vegetarian corpses before I make the
requisite level, though.  You don't get disintegration
resistance without breaking conduct, but who cares?
Reflection (which you need for other reasons) has
you covered anyway.  Permanent invisibility also
means breaking conduct (I think), but again, it's not
a particularly important intrinsic to acquire.  Teleport
control is probably the toughest intrinsic (you may
have to break conduct *repeatedly* by eating any
number of tengu before you get it), but you can
use a ring for that in a pinch, and anyway teleport
control is more of a convenience than a necessity
most of the time.

> Credit cloning helps.

I've never had the patience for that.

Also, call me silly, but it would tend to bother my
scruples.  (This is odd, since using a magic whistle
to eliminate inconvenient shopkeepers doesn't seem
to bother me.  Maybe it's that killing, however wrong
it would be IRL, feels more like an inherent aspect
of the game; credit cloning feels more like exploiting
of the limitations of the shopkeeper code.)

> Martial Arts at Grand Master seemed to do a fair
> amount of damage.

In the early game, yeah.  I haven't got far enough
(as a monk) to evaluate it in the late game.

> My spellbook was protection.  perhaps it would have
> been my 3rd choice out of sleep, healing, and protection,

Second choice for me.  Sleep is one of the most common
wands, and I find I always have a surplus of them.

> regardless of which one you get, don't forget to use it.  I found it
> so much easier to fight elf-lords at AC -7 than AC 0.

How do you manage to actually finish the fight before the
protection wears off?  You only get, like, three turns...  I
find buying protection from an aligned priest to be a rather
more effective means of acquiring it.

> My biggest concern in the middle game was a lack of
> MR.  I think I got a throne wish or magic lamp wish at
> some point for the EoA.

Yeah, my plan as a neutral monk is to wish for the Eye if
at all possible, or as a lawful monk the magic mirror.  That
probably means I have to make it to the castle in order to
get MR (unless the RNG just decides to do me a big favor),
but that's true for a lot of roles.

I haven't thought yet about how I'd handle MR as a chaotic
monk.  Chaotic alignment in general is something I haven't
really learned to deal with well yet.

> Once I got to XL 14 or so, I reverse genocided some
> silver dragons and played my monk like I play any
> other character:  SDSM, MR artifact, and +7 artifact
> weapon.  At that point, the game becomes easy again.

Interesting.  All the good artifact weapons are going to
be unskilled forever, aren't they?  Or can you get one
as a gift and get it unrestricted?

Of course, the really good artifacts have a +d5 to-hit
bonus against more or less everything.  A lawful monk
would have particularly good options in this regard (at
the expense of not being able to touch the Eye).

> Before I got the EoA, I think I mostly wore a dwarvish cloak around.
> When hard monsters showed up, I'd retreat to the previous level, put
> on my robe, cast protection, put the cloak back on, and then go
> fight.

I've been sticking with the robe, on the grounds that
it provides MC3.  Does the dwarvish cloak have some
advantage versus the robe that I'm not aware of?

> Incidentally, don't worry about what you eat;
> the penalty for eating meat is trivial.

I've been eating a *mostly* vegetarian diet as a monk
but breaking the conduct occasionally when I have a
significant motive for doing so, e.g., one floating eye
for the telepathy.  If I experienced a serious food
shortage I would probably eat meat before praying
for nutrition -- pending the *availability* of meat at
the time; I've found that the RNG has a disturbing
habit of generating lots of monsters when I'm not
hungry or have plenty of food or am fainting but
generating NO monsters when I'm merely very
hungry or weak and have no food.  I assume this
is just random chance, not programmed-in, but it
has nonetheless resulted in my death on a good
number of occasions, playing various roles (monk,
tourist, any role that tends to run low on food).

I have *not* had hunger issues when playing as
a tank (Barbarian, Valkyrie), probably because
the need to retreat and heal dominates much
less of my time when playing those roles.  A
monk with the healing spell, similarly, has few
hunger problems, because he doesn't have to
spend tens of thousands of turns retreating.
Tourist is the role that gives me the most
hunger issues, of the roles I've played so far.


